{"kumba": [{"id": "kumba", "headword": "कुम्ब", "headword_slp1": "kumba", "fold": "kumba", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumba is mentioned with opaśa and kurīra as an ornament of women's hair in the Atharvaveda. Geldner thinks that, like those two words, it originally meant ‘horn,’ but this is very doubtful. Indian tradition simply regards the term as denoting a female adornment connected with the dressing of the hair.\nFoot notes vi. 138, 3.\nVedische Studien, 1, 131.\nSāyaṇa on Av. vi. 138, 3.\nCf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 265; Bloomfield, Hymns of the Atharvaveda, 538, 539; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 348; Caland, Über das vituclle sūtra des baudhāyana, 59.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["kumba", "कुंब", "కుంబ", "కుమ్బ", "ಕುಂಬ", "ಕುಮ್ಬ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["kumba", "kuMba", "కుంబ", "కుమ్బ", "ಕುಂಬ", "ಕುಮ್ಬ"]}], "kumbya": [{"id": "kumbyA", "headword": "कुम्ब्या", "headword_slp1": "kumbyA", "fold": "kumbya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumbyā or kumvyā is a word mentioned after Ṛc, yajus, sāman, and gāthā in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa to denote a form of speech. In the aitareya Āraṇyaka it appears as one of the forms of measured speech together with Ṛc and gāthā. The precise meaning of the term is unknown. Weber suggests the sense ‘refrain.’\nFoot notes xi. 5, 7, 10.\nii. 3, 6.\nIndische Studien, 10, 111, n.\nCf. Keith, aitareya Āraṇyaka, 221; Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East, 44. 101.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["kumbyaa", "kumbyā", "kumvyaa", "kumvyā", "कुंब्या", "कुम्व्या", "కుంబ్యా", "కుమ్బ్యా", "కుమ్వ్యా", "ಕುಂಬ್ಯಾ", "ಕುಮ್ಬ್ಯಾ", "ಕುಮ್ವ್ಯಾ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["kumbyaa", "kumbyā", "kumvyaa", "kumvyā", "kuMbyA", "kumvyA", "కుంబ్యా", "కుమ్బ్యా", "కుమ్వ్యా", "ಕುಂಬ್ಯಾ", "ಕುಮ್ಬ್ಯಾ", "ಕುಮ್ವ್ಯಾ"]}], "kumBa": [{"id": "kumBa", "headword": "कुम्भ", "headword_slp1": "kumBa", "fold": "kumBa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumbha is a word of frequent occurrence in the Rigveda, as well as later, and denotes a ‘pot.’ Usually no doubt made of clay, it was easily broken. See also ukhā.\nFoot notes i. 116, 7; 117, 6; vii. 33, 13, etc.\nAv. i. 6, 4; iii. 12, 7, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 87, etc.\nRv. x. 89, 7.\nCf. Schrader, Prehistoric Antiquities, 367.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["kumbha", "कुंभ", "కుంభ", "కుమ్భ", "ಕುಂಭ", "ಕುಮ್ಭ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["kumbha", "kuMBa", "కుంభ", "కుమ్భ", "ಕುಂಭ", "ಕುಮ್ಭ"]}], "kumBi-nasa": [{"id": "kumBI-nasa", "headword": "कुम्भी-नस", "headword_slp1": "kumBI-nasa", "fold": "kumBi-nasa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumbhī-nasa (‘pot-nosed’) is the name of an animal mentioned in the list of victims at the horse sacrifice (aśvamedha) in the taittirīya Saṃhitā. Possibly some sort of snake is meant, as in the later literature.\nFoot notes v. 5, 14, 1. Cf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 95; St. Petersburg Dictionary, s. v.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["kumbhee", "kumbhee-nasa", "kumbheenasa", "kumbhi", "kumbhii", "kumbhii-nasa", "kumbhiinasa", "kumbhī", "kumbhī-nasa", "kumbhīnasa", "nasa", "कुंभि", "कुंभी", "कुंभी-नस", "कुंभीनस", "कुम्भि", "कुम्भी", "कुम्भीनस", "नस", "కుంభి", "కుంభీ", "కుంభీ-నస", "కుంభీనస", "కుమ్భి", "కుమ్భీ", "కుమ్భీ-నస", "కుమ్భీనస", "నస", "ಕುಂಭಿ", "ಕುಂಭೀ", "ಕುಂಭೀ-ನಸ", "ಕುಂಭೀನಸ", "ಕುಮ್ಭಿ", "ಕುಮ್ಭೀ", "ಕುಮ್ಭೀ-ನಸ", "ಕುಮ್ಭೀನಸ", "ನಸ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["kumbhee", "kumbhee-nasa", "kumbheenasa", "kumbhi", "kumbhii", "kumbhii-nasa", "kumbhiinasa", "kumbhī", "kumbhī-nasa", "kumbhīnasa", "nasa", "kuMBi", "kuMBI", "kuMBI-nasa", "kuMBInasa", "kumBi", "kumBI", "kumBInasa", "nasa", "కుంభి", "కుంభీ", "కుంభీ-నస", "కుంభీనస", "కుమ్భి", "కుమ్భీ", "కుమ్భీ-నస", "కుమ్భీనస", "నస", "ಕುಂಭಿ", "ಕುಂಭೀ", "ಕುಂಭೀ-ನಸ", "ಕುಂಭೀನಸ", "ಕುಮ್ಭಿ", "ಕುಮ್ಭೀ", "ಕುಮ್ಭೀ-ನಸ", "ಕುಮ್ಭೀನಸ", "ನಸ"]}], "kumala-barhis": [{"id": "kumala-barhis", "headword": "कुमल-बर्हिस्", "headword_slp1": "kumala-barhis", "fold": "kumala-barhis", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumala-barhis is mentioned in the pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa (xv. 3, 21) as the seer of a sāman or Chant.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["barhi", "barhis", "kumala", "kumala-barhi", "kumala-barhis", "kumalabarhi", "kumalabarhis", "कुमल", "कुमल-बर्हि", "कुमलबर्हि", "कुमलबर्हिस्", "बर्हि", "बर्हिस्", "కుమల", "కుమల-బర్హి", "కుమల-బర్హిస్", "కుమలబర్హి", "కుమలబర్హిస్", "బర్హి", "బర్హిస్", "ಕುಮಲ", "ಕುಮಲ-ಬರ್ಹಿ", "ಕುಮಲ-ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್", "ಕುಮಲಬರ್ಹಿ", "ಕುಮಲಬರ್ಹಿಸ್", "ಬರ್ಹಿ", "ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್"], "synonyms_slp1": ["barhi", "barhis", "kumala", "kumala-barhi", "kumala-barhis", "kumalabarhi", "kumalabarhis", "kumala", "kumala-barhi", "kumalabarhi", "kumalabarhis", "barhi", "barhis", "కుమల", "కుమల-బర్హి", "కుమల-బర్హిస్", "కుమలబర్హి", "కుమలబర్హిస్", "బర్హి", "బర్హిస్", "ಕುಮಲ", "ಕುಮಲ-ಬರ್ಹಿ", "ಕುಮಲ-ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್", "ಕುಮಲಬರ್ಹಿ", "ಕುಮಲಬರ್ಹಿಸ್", "ಬರ್ಹಿ", "ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್"]}], "kumara harita": [{"id": "kumAra hArita", "headword": "कुमार हारित", "headword_slp1": "kumAra hArita", "fold": "kumara harita", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumāra Hārita is mentioned in the first vaṃśa (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad as a pupil of gālava.\nFoot notes ii. 5, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3, kāṇva).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["kumaara haarita", "kumāra hārita", "కుమార హారిత", "ಕುಮಾರ ಹಾರಿತ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["kumaara haarita", "kumāra hārita", "కుమార హారిత", "ಕುಮಾರ ಹಾರಿತ"]}], "kumara-harita": [{"id": "kumAra-hArita", "headword": "कुमार-हारित", "headword_slp1": "kumAra-hArita", "fold": "kumara-harita", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Kumāra-hārita, 1, 172, should come after kubhra, 1, 162.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["haarita", "hārita", "kumaara", "kumaara-haarita", "kumaarahaarita", "kumāra", "kumā